  o Save and bones files with another version, save revision or critical
    byte count are refused. Restore does not convert or repair an earlier
    layout. sfctool requires the same version and revision before
    converting between machine formats. Both recovery paths reject a
    different critical byte count, layout, revision or version, and an
    invalid player-name length, before writing a save or deleting any
    checkpoint files. These checks are shared with the recover utility.

  o A refused save header is kept readable and recompressed. The game
    explains the refusal, asks the player to tell the operator, and exits
    with failure without starting a new game under the same name. The
    same path handles a mismatched seed generator version or invalid seed.
    Header count, version and revision refusals include the file and build
    values. Interrupts cannot enter gameplay or write a score while the
    refusal is displayed.

  o Only a seeded game's save file differs from NetHack 5.0's. It's marked
    by a feature bit of its own in the version info (SEEDED_GAME_BIT, set by
    store_version() for a seeded game's save file and checkpoint), and holds
    the seed data below at the start of its game state (savegamestate()).
    NetHack 5.0 doesn't know the bit, so it rejects such a file as a
    configuration it wasn't built with ("Configuration incompatibility"),
    whatever its VERSION_COMPATIBILITY; this fork lists the bit among the
    features it ignores when checking (md_ignored_features()), and
    uptodate() tells the restore whether the file has it (NHFILE's seeded
    field, which restgamestate() reads; check_version() changes nothing
    else). sfctool keeps the bit when it converts a file, and reads the
    seed data by it. An unseeded game's save file, and bones (seeded games
    make none), are the same as the matching upstream version's, both ways.

  o The seed is saved with the game (gamestate-seed), with whether it was
    the server's hidden seed (gamestate-seedhidden). A restored game keeps
    its own seed, and stays hidden or not, whatever the option or sysconf
    says; a game started without a seed stays unseeded. If the option was
    ignored, the welcome message says so (for a hidden seed, whether the
    option happened to match it isn't let on). This stops a player restoring
    under a different seed to reroll unvisited levels, or restoring an
    unseeded game as a seeded one, and a hidden seed from being shown when
    its game is restored without SEED.

  o A saved seed is checked as the option's value is (B1), and must already
    be in canonical form. An invalid seed keeps the save and exits without
    starting a new game ("The saved game's seed isn't valid."). A build
    without USE_ISAAC64 refuses seeded saves the same way.

  o If a failed restore starts a new game, it uses the seed sysconf and the
    options set (B3), not the one the failed restore read.

  o The seed generator version (gamestate-seedver), every level's
    fingerprint (gamestate-levelhash) and every level's terrain as it was
    made (gamestate-layout: for each ledger, whether there is one, then
    COLNO * ROWNO cells packed as LAYOUT_TERR(); the layout the live feed's
    keyframes are written against, B14) are saved too. sfctool copies the
    terrain through a scratch buffer. Level files and bones hold none. The
    saved generator version must match SEED_GEN_VERSION to restore. A
    mismatch shows both versions, keeps the save readable, asks the player
    to tell the operator, and exits without starting a new game. This
    check runs when a save is selected; the saved-game menu checks only
    its header. The current version is shown and logged; a game cannot
    span different generator versions.

