  o Recording: with RECORDFILE set in sysconf (a path, with DUMPLOGFILE's
    placeholders), a seeded game played with the tty interface, in a build
    with DUMPLOG, writes to that file everything it takes from outside: its
    command-line arguments (process_options()), the login name it took the
    hero's name from (whoami()), the options it parsed (the options file's
    text as read, noted by parse_conf_file(), and NETHACKOPTIONS), every
    random seed it draws from the system (init_random(), reseed_random()),
    every key it reads from the terminal (tty_nhgetch()), the answer each
    time sysconf's WIZARDS, EXPLORERS or SHELLERS decides whether the
    player may do something (check_user_string(): a replay runs as the
    operator, whom those may allow or not), and where it acted on a hangup
    or an interrupt. Whether it's a tty game is settled once the options
    are read, since they can choose the interface (windowtype); from then
    on keys and signals are recorded. Besides those it writes a digest of
    the game's state (the turn, the hero's position, vital statistics and
    inventory, and the current level's contents) every 100 turns, on
    arriving on a level, and when a session ends (once a save has
    succeeded, or at the end of the game). The game's own randomness is
    unchanged; the seeds are only noted, so play still can't be practised.
    A game running without permissions the player lacks can also be
    recorded to the file NH_RECORD names (with the same placeholders),
    e.g. on the player's own machine; NH_RECORD is ignored when RECORDFILE
    is set.

  o The record file: a new game creates it and never overwrites one that
    exists ("%d" in the path, the game's start, keeps games apart); a
    restored game appends to it; neither follows a symbolic link. If a
    seeded game can't be recorded where it should be, it doesn't go on: a
    new one ends before anything is shown, a restored one is saved again
    and ends, telling the player to tell the operator (an unrecorded game
    couldn't be told from one whose record was held back;
    nhrec_refuse()). A write that fails later (a full disk, say) ends the
    game as on a hangup, saved, at the next point one is acted on, and
    says why on exit (nhrec_write_failed()). A refused restore in explore
    or debug mode keeps the save file the restore kept.

  o Format: a series of entries, each "TAG LENGTH:PAYLOAD" and a newline,
    where LENGTH is the payload's length in bytes, so options of any length,
    with newlines, go in and come out exactly. Each session (from starting
    or restoring the game to saving it or its end) starts with its header:
    session (new or restore), name, mode, term (the terminal's size), seed
    (as shown to players; never a hidden seed itself), reseed (whether the
    game reseeds at level changes, which depends on the system having a
    strong source of random numbers), login, one arg per argument, rcfile
    and envopts. Then its events: s (a seed), k (a key), hup and intr (a
    hangup or an interrupt, see below), c (a checkpoint's digest), end (the
    session's end and its digest). files.c's comment lists them. Events
    read before it's known whether the game is recorded (the first seeds,
    keys at startup) are kept until then; a new game is known from just
    after u_init_misc() in newgame() (before anything about it is shown), a
    restored one from welcome().

  o Signals: in a game that may be recorded (from when the options start
    to be read, until it's known it isn't) or is replayed, the hangup and
    interrupt handlers (hangup(), done1()) only note the signal
    (program_state.pending_hup, pending_intr), and keys are read with
    poll() and read(), which a signal wakes (tty_poll_getch()). The
    game acts on a noted signal at one of two points, and records it with
    where: at a key read (nhrec_key(): the hangup instead of the key, or the
    interrupt handler before it), or at the start of a pass of
    moveloop_core() (nhrec_poll(): a hangup or interrupt that arrives in the
    middle of a long search, a run or travel, recorded with the pass's
    number, counted from the session's start). A replay acts on it at the
    same point, not before. Another ^C while one is being answered is
    dropped. An interrupt is done_interrupt(), what done1() does when the
    game isn't recorded. It normally offers to quit; the managed-session
    policy (B13) refuses quit without a prompt.

  o Replay: "nethack --replay FILE" (Unix; nhrec_replay_args() takes the
    arguments before anything else) reads the whole record, rejecting any
    entry that isn't well formed (nhrec_load()), and plays its first
    session: the game takes the session's arguments, login name, options
    (the recorded options file, written to the scratch playground as
    replay.nethackrc, and NETHACKOPTIONS), seeds, keys and signals from
    it, reseeding where the recorded game did, and has the recorded
    terminal's size (nhrec_term_size(), used by getwindowsz()) whatever
    the real one is. It runs in a scratch playground (nhrec_enter_scratch():
    a directory under TMPDIR with links to the playground's read-only
    files, and fresh empty logs and save directory), so its saves and
    level files can't touch the player's; sysconf is the installed one,
    but RECORDFILE, DUMPLOGFILE, MSGHANDLER and CRASHREPORTURL are ignored
    while replaying, and so is SEED for a record of a player's own seed
    (nhrec_sysconf_ignored()). A record of a server's hidden seed needs
    that seed, from sysconf or --seed. It checks that its seed is the
    recorded one as soon as the options are read, that the rest of the
    header matches when the game starts, and each digest (so a replay
    shows the game stayed the same all along, and where it didn't), and
    it stops as soon as the game has ended, before the xlogfile, the high
    score list or a dumplog would be written. The files the replay
    writes in the scratch playground (replay.nethackrc, replay.results,
    seedfuzz.txt) are never linked from the playground, and are opened
    without following links (nhrec_scratch_file()). A payload with a
    '\0' (other than an options file's text) or a number over its range
    is rejected.

  o Watching: between keys the replay waits (100 ms at first) and reads
    the real keyboard (nhrec_pace()): space pauses and resumes, "." plays
    one key and pauses, "+" and "-" halve and double the wait, ">" runs
    flat out, and "q" stops the replay. Nothing is drawn for these, since
    drawing would take recorded keys. With --verify there's no waiting,
    and animation delays are skipped (tty_delay_output()).

  o Sessions: a record of a game that was saved and restored has one
    session per run of the game. When a session's replay ends with a save,
    or was cut off where the recorded game was killed, the next session
    is run in the same scratch playground by a fresh process
    (nhrec_next_session(): execv() of this program with --session N and
    --scratch DIR), as the game was played; after a failed session the
    rest aren't replayed. After a cut-off session the replay first does
    what the server did before the next one: if that session restored a
    game, it recovers the checkpoint files into a save file itself
    (recover_savefile(), what the recover utility does), otherwise it
    clears them (a restarted game does that itself when the old process
    is gone, but the replay's next process has the same id, since
    execv() keeps it, and would be offered to recover instead). The
    recovered state is the last checkpoint the replayed session wrote,
    which is the killed game's unless it was killed in the middle of a
    turn, after reading a key and before that turn's checkpoint. The
    last process shows every session's outcome ("verified", "failed:
    ...", "cut off: ..." or "stopped: ..."), removes the scratch
    playground (it's kept, and named, after a failure) and exits with 0
    if the whole record checked out, 1 if a session failed, 2 if one was
    cut off or stopped. A replay needs a build for the same kind of
    system as the recorded game's (a 64-bit system's seeds don't fit a
    32-bit build), and the player's own permissions.

  o test/replaytest.py checks recording and replay over a long game: in a
    copy of the playground it plays a recorded seeded game with thousands
    of random ordinary keys (in wizard mode also teleporting between
    levels), saving and restoring it several times, then replays it with
    "nethack --replay FILE --verify"; with
    --signals it also interrupts the game now and then, ends sessions with a
    hangup in the middle of a long search, and quits with ^C.
    test/recordfail.py checks a game whose record can't be written (its
    header, on /dev/full; a later key, past the file size limit) ends
    saved, that a replay leaves those three files in the playground
    alone, and that a record with an out-of-range value or a '\0' is
    rejected.

  o What else would make a replay differ is kept out of recorded games:
    mail isn't delivered (it comes from outside), the game doesn't follow a
    change of the terminal's size (it decides where messages break, and so
    which keys are read), and there's no shell escape (what's typed to the
    shell isn't recorded) and no suspending (the terminal can change
    meanwhile).

  o Keep records away from players until the race is over: they show every
    move of the game.


  +-------------------+
  | B12. Live feed    |
  +-------------------+

  o With the Unix tty port, NETHACK_FEED_FD names an inherited descriptor
    (ignored unless real and effective user and group IDs match) to which the
    game writes JSON lines: the hero, level, objects, monsters, messages,
    keys, menus, effects, events and the configured end-of-game dump.
    This includes unseen terrain and unidentified objects, so a race's
    feed is for its collector, kept away from players until the race is
    over, like its records.
    Other window ports, including curses, disable the feed. Replay's next
    session inherits the descriptor; unrelated execs close it. A closed
    pipe stops the feed without leaving SIGPIPE ignored in executed tools.
    A slow collector blocks the game until it drains the pipe; interrupt
    and hangup handling waits for a pending write to finish. SIGPIPE is
    caught process-wide when the feed opens (also if a non-tty port later
    disables it), and SIGUSR1 is caught even in games without a feed.
    Xcode includes the feed implementation in its game target.

  o Changes are written at command boundaries, during multi-turn
    actions, and whenever the game is about to wait for a key (a --More--,
    a prompt, a menu, line input), so the feed written before a wait is
    the game as it waits: a snapshot taken there (a managed fork) starts
    from exactly the state the feed describes. While waiting, nothing is
    written unless something changed: a hero line only when the hero did,
    a "chk" line only after something new. Full keyframes are written on
    level arrival, periodically (NETHACK_FEED_KF_EVERY), and when SIGUSR1
    asks for one. Tty input uses poll() for feed-enabled games, recorded
    or not, so requests are served while waiting at commands and prompts
    too. During naming, a level transition or a panic the feed doesn't
    bring itself up to date at a wait, and requests wait until it can
    safely take a snapshot; the level event precedes its arrival keyframe.
    A game that ends, is saved or is hung up writes a last keyframe of the
    state it ended in ("death", "end"), the save's before the level and
    inventory are freed; it answers a request still waiting, and nothing
    about the game's state follows it, even if the save then fails. There
    is none after a panic, a fatal error, a replay that stops early, or a
    hangup in the middle of a line. A hangup ends the wait for a key in an
    unrecorded game with the feed too, as the read it interrupts does
    without the feed.

  o Glyph metadata doesn't depend on where a glyph is or on the player's
    options: default characters and colours (as if the color option were
    on), no accessibility overrides. The hero's "screen" tuple [glyph,
    character, color] carries the styling at the hero's position,
    including showrace colour, in both hero lines and keyframes. Text
    windows and messages written with putmixed() carry decoded symbols,
    not \G glyph escapes.
    Strings preserve valid UTF-8; other non-ASCII bytes are represented as
    U+00XX characters, as for Latin-1 text. Menu items carry the selectors
    assigned by tty, including its automatic letters.
    Menus emit a "menu_open" event before waiting for input, followed by
    the "menu" result event; "win" identifies the window in both.
    Keyframes include the open menus in "menus", with their prompts,
    selection modes and items, so a collector can recover a pending menu.
    Kill events carry "phase" (hero or monster movement), not attribution
    of the kill.

  o A keyframe's "level" names the level's layout (B14) by its hash
    ("layout"; null in an unseeded game and in wizard mode) and gives only
    the runs of cells whose terrain differs from it ("terr": [i, "typ",
    "flags", "lit", "horiz"], a character per cell; with no layout, from
    all stone). The remembered map is a palette ("sym", [glyph, "ch",
    color, "what"] in order of first appearance, the unexplored glyph
    included) and a code of one or two base64 characters per cell ("g",
    "gw"); the screen is listed only where it differs from the map ("scr",
    [i, glyph]); the cells in sight are runs ("vis", [i, n]). Traps,
    engravings, stairs, rooms, objects and monsters are whole. Water and
    Air are diffed against their layout like any other level.

  o NH_FEEDCHECK (a test hook, ignored unless real and effective user and
    group IDs match, as for NH_STATELOG): after each point where the feed
    brings itself up to date (every feed_sync() at a command boundary or
    during a multi-turn action, one at a wait that wrote something, the
    end of feed_level_leave(), and feed_last()'s last keyframe on a
    death, a save or a hangup), a "chk" line with FNV-1a 64
    hashes of the level as it really is: its terrain, remembered map,
    screen and cells in sight (feed_check()). test/layouttest.py folds
    feeds against them; test/feedtest.py turns them on in every run with
    the feed.

  o The feed only observes. Object naming preserves the name buffers and
    knowledge bits, and uses quietnaming to suppress side effects and RNG
    draws. Unpaid price suffixes are omitted: looking up an unpaid price
    can apply a shopkeeper's anger surcharge. The object's "unpaid" field
    still reports its status. Other items retain remembered price quotes
    when the pricequotes option is on; those only read the object table.
    Floor sale prices aren't requested by the feed's doname() calls.
    Eroded shirt and smock text is omitted even at game end because
    producing it can draw random numbers. Monster names come from tables;
    "shk" is the shopkeeper's name and "shopkeeper" its boolean marker.
    The feed copies a dumplog only when one is configured: generating an
    extra dump would itself change state and draw random numbers before
    an unseeded game's bones are made.

  o NH_STATELOG names a diagnostic file for per-key state hashes and core
    and display RNG draw counts (with or without ISAAC64). Like NH_RECORD,
    it is ignored unless real and effective user and group IDs match.
    test/feedtest.py compares games and replays with the feed on
    and off, including signals; the record's digests alone do not cover
    every state change or RNG draw that naming could cause.
    Linux CI uses the wizard policy to exercise level changes and naming
    cases; test/feedfaults.py also checks interrupts during a partial
    write and that the feed does not cause an extra dump to be generated.
    Its interrupt fixture waits at a command boundary, then for the feed
    pipe to fill and for the quit prompt, without a search that can leave
    a monster-discovery prompt pending.
    It also checks that observing an unpaid object leaves the shop bill
    and surcharge flag unchanged, which the diagnostic hashes don't cover.
    Remembered quotes are checked in the feed's names themselves.


  +----------------------------------+
  | B13. Managed terminal sessions    |
  +----------------------------------+

Files: include/hack.h, sys/unix/unixmain.c, src/end.c, src/save.c,
dat/usagehlp, doc/nethack.6, doc/Guidebook.*, sys/unix/sysconf,
test/sessiontest.py, test/launchtest.py, test/feedgame.py

  o On Unix, --managed-session lets a trusted launcher refuse explicit
    quit and save-and-exit. process_options() reads it after nhrec_args(),
    so a replay takes the recorded policy. It lives in program_state,
    never the save file or player options. A new process must receive the
    flag again, including on restore; a live VM fork inherits it.

  o done2() refuses before tutorial and confirmation prompts, using the
    declined-quit cleanup to cancel running actions and rearm SIGINT.
    This also prevents using #quit to leave the tutorial for normal play.
    dosave() refuses the player's save command; dosave0(), which hangup
    saving uses, doesn't refuse.
    Refusals print a message without taking a turn or drawing RNG, and
    override MSGTYPE rules so a refusal doesn't ask for another key.
    The quit message is flushed before a raw interrupt returns to the
    key read already in progress.
    Rebound commands and raw or deferred interrupts use the same guards.

  o The policy is independent of seeds and the live feed. Death, escape,
    ascension, EOF, hangup and recovery behave as without it.
    A broker keeps the PTY master across client disconnects; the supervisor
    can close it to hang up, save and exit. The deployment keeps SHELLERS,
    WIZARDS and EXPLORERS empty in trusted sysconf, the settings that
    control shell escapes, suspending and special modes. Permissions and
    broker access remain the launcher's responsibility.

  o test/sessiontest.py checks command aliases, repeated interrupts and
    literal Ctrl-C, unchanged turns and RNG counters, hangup saving,
    restore with and without the policy, normal escape and recorded replay.
    It waits for the first command boundary before sampling, excluding
    startup RNG draws, and for each sampled Escape to reach the state log.
    The policy is in neither the record's format and digests nor save
    files.

  o test/launchtest.py checks normal seeded games with a trusted launcher,
    strict sysconf, clean environment, descriptor 3 and terminal settings.
    It checks both layout forms, early and idle snapshots, and trusted
    shutdown during gameplay, menus, line input and death disclosure.
    The gameplay case also checks mode and managed-session refusals.
    A signal sent on receiving the header can be satisfied by the initial
    "arrive" keyframe; a separate "signal" frame is not required. Later
    idle requests require a "signal" frame. A prompt case swaps places with
    the pet, which an options file's MSGTYPE makes wait at --More-- in the
    middle of the turn: a snapshot there must equal the feed folded up to
    the wait, the pet already moved. The game randomly refuses one swap
    in seven and makes the pet flee; the test waits for the next command,
    refreshes its snapshot, and waits for the pet to return if necessary.
    Only that explicit refusal permits another attempt; a missing swap
    prompt or a mismatched snapshot still fails, and a successful swap
    is required within 40 commands. Final feeds reconstruct against the
    layouts, every keyframe on a level already described equals the
    folded state, and every recording verifies.


  +----------------------+
  | B14. Level layouts   |
  +----------------------+

Files: src/mklev.c, src/feed.c, src/display.c, src/files.c, src/wizcmds.c,
src/allmain.c, src/cfgfiles.c, src/windows.c, src/end.c, src/pline.c,
src/do_name.c, src/save.c, src/restore.c, include/extern.h, include/rm.h,
sys/unix/unixmain.c,
dat/usagehlp, doc/nethack.6, test/layouttest.py, test/seedfuzz.py

  o A level's layout is what every game with the seed has on it, whatever
    the player did and in whatever order the levels are visited: every
    cell's terrain (typ, flags, lit, horizontal), the traps layout_trap()
    accepts (position, type, seen, destination), engravings (position,
    type, text, read), stairs and ladders (position, up, ladder,
    destination), rooms (bounds, rtype, lit), the special level's name and
    its flags (hardfloor, noteleport, maze, nommap, shortsighted,
    graveyard, can dig down, can fall through). Objects and monsters
    aren't part of it: A2's differences reach them, and their ids count
    everything made before.

  o layout_trap() leaves out a web that the pending-web loop in mklev()
    put under a room's giant spider (genocide decides whether the spider
    is there; level scripts' webs stay), and the Fort Ludios portal in a
    vault, whose level is the first one made that could have it. The
    portal on the Fort Ludios level stays, but where it leads (back to
    that vault) counts as nowhere set (-1, -1).

  o At the end of mklev(), the level's layout hash (64 bits, salted as the
    fingerprints are, B8) is computed by layout_hash() and becomes part 0
    of its fingerprint as made; and in a seeded game its terrain is kept
    (gseed.layout[], COLNO * ROWNO cells packed as LAYOUT_TERR(), as the
    feed packs it), saved with the game (B7). The traps, engravings and
    stairs are sorted by cell (layout_traps(), layout_engravings(),
    layout_stairs()) for the hash and the dump alike.

  o "nethack --layouts FILE" ("-": standard output) writes every level's
    layout as JSON lines and exits: hdr, symbols, dungeon (dungeons,
    branches, and the levels whose vault would get the Fort Ludios
    portal), one level line per level in ledger order with its terrain
    as made and its traps, engravings, stairs and rooms in the keyframes'
    encoding, a skip line for the tutorial's and the endgame placeholder's
    ledgers, and an end line with the count and an FNV-1a 64 hash of every byte
    before it (feed.c's comment has the fields). "--layout-hashes FILE"
    writes the hdr, each level's dn, dl and layout, and the same end line:
    both come from one run of the same code, so the hashes always agree.
    Unix only. The output depends only on the seed, SEED_GEN_VERSION, the
    build and the data files.

  o The full dump's "symbols" line describes the canonical generated
    view: default characters and default colours on every level,
    including Rogue. Every symbol is [glyph, "character", color,
    "explanation"], already resolved; readers need no glyph offsets or
    compiled drawing tables. The full hdr's "symbols" is the FNV-1a 64
    hash of that entire line, including its newline, for caching.
    The hash-only form has neither field nor line.
    Readers of the older full format must accept and retain the new
    symbols line before consuming dumps from this build.

    "unexplored" is the GLYPH_UNEXPLORED tuple, the same as in a game's
    keyframe palette: a space, NO_COLOR (8), and an empty explanation.
    Its glyph number is build-specific; readers use this entry to mask
    unexplored cells or draw a level before its first keyframe arrives.

    "terrain" is indexed by typ. Each entry is an ordered array of
    rules: choose the first matching rule and use its "sym" tuple.
    All conditions in a rule must match; an omitted condition imposes
    no restriction. "flags": [mask, value] matches when the cell's
    flags & mask equals value. "horiz", "lit", "arboreal" and
    "arboreal_sdoor" match integer 0 or 1; "wallset" matches a string.
    typ and flags decode as character code minus 'A'; lit and horiz
    decode as character code minus '0'. These are not base64 strings.

    Each full level line supplies "wallset" ("main", "mines",
    "gehennom", "ludios", "sokoban"), "arboreal" (0 or 1), and
    "arboreal_sdoors" (cell indices, in ascending order). Membership
    in that list supplies a cell's arboreal_sdoor condition. Wall sets
    follow In_mines, In_hell, Is_knox, In_sokoban in that order, with
    main as the fallback, including Vlad's Tower. These fields are
    outside the terrain strings and individual layout hashes; the
    full dump's end checksum covers them and the symbols line.

    Backgrounds match back_to_glyph() with seenv = SVALL, waslit = lit,
    and no known branch stairs. Fully known walls show their own
    junctions regardless of wall mode. Room floor always uses S_room;
    corridors use S_litcorr exactly when lit. Doors use state and
    orientation; secret doors remain walls, except arboreal secret
    doors, which are trees even off arboreal levels (garden rooms).
    Stone and secret corridors are trees on arboreal levels. DBWALL
    is the raised bridge; DRAWBRIDGE_UP is its open span, showing the
    underlying moat, lava, ice or room floor. Invalid drawbridge underlay
    bits fall back to room floor, as back_to_glyph() does after its
    diagnostic. Stairs and ladders use plain up/down symbols. Altars
    select sanctum first regardless of alignment, then lawful, neutral,
    chaotic and unaligned as fallback; the shrine bit has no effect.

    Apply overlays with full knowledge, in this order:
      1. A dumped trap, unless any rule in "covers_traps" matches.
         Those rules use typ equality and the same masked flags test.
         They cover pool, moat, water, lava, lava wall and a raised
         drawbridge span over moat, with no underwater hero condition.
         "traps" is indexed by ttyp (entry 0 is null), including the
         detected door and chest trap symbols at 24 and 25, although
         these are not generated map traps and never appear in a dump's
         trap list.
         Juiblex's level has no drawbridges: were one added over moat,
         is_moat() would exclude its raised span from trap coverage there.
         Direct MOAT cells still cover traps through is_pool(). The
         oracle checks this exception and requires that level to remain
         free of raised drawbridges unless the dump rules are extended.
      2. Otherwise a dumped engraving, if "engravings"[typ] is not
         null: use that tuple. Only corridor, room and ice qualify;
         corridor uses S_engrcorr, room and ice S_engroom. A grave's
         headstone keeps the grave background.
      3. Otherwise the background from "terrain".
    Trap seen and engraving revealed/read bits do not affect this view.
    Monsters and objects are outside its scope. This is not magic
    mapping, which discovers secret corridors and prioritizes furniture.

  o NH_LAYOUTCHECK names a test-only JSON-lines output file explicitly
    honoured in dump mode. Each line has k "oracle", dn, dl and "cells":
    a canonical symbol tuple for every cell. It calls back_to_glyph()
    and the game's overlay rules independently of the exported table,
    temporarily setting full knowledge and restoring the cells and
    stair knowledge afterwards. layout_glyphmap() uses the display's
    glyph-map builder with default colours, bypassing the window port's
    colour capabilities and Rogue styling, then restores the exact
    glyph map, flags and timestamp. Both the symbols and the oracle use
    this mode; ordinary display and live-feed colour handling is unchanged.
    Each line also has "overlays": [typ, flags, covers_traps,
    shows_engravings] tuples for every terrain type and all 32 flag
    values, evaluated by the game's predicates on a scratch cell and
    then restoring that cell. This checks the exported overlay rules
    even for combinations absent from generated levels.
    The hook writes to a regular file made private (0600) before writing,
    including an existing file; with O_NOFOLLOW, symlinks are refused.
    Relative paths are resolved from the caller's directory, before
    changing to the scratch playground; paths
    may exceed nh_getenv's 128-byte option limit. An invalid path is an
    error, never silently ignored. layouttest.py compares every cell
    with a dump-only reader over six seeds. It requires coverage of all
    wall sets, door and altar cases, dumped
    trap types, a Ranger's arboreal start, garden secret doors, branch
    stairs and engravings, and checks dump bytes are identical with and
    without the hook.
    Fixed character, colour and explanation anchors check the rules
    independently of the oracle, including trees, lava, lit corridors,
    sanctum altars, ice engravings, covered traps and trap precedence.
    Keyframes check the unexplored tuple, including its build-specific
    number. New and existing diagnostic files, relative and long paths,
    and invalid paths are checked too.

  o How it runs: the arguments are read first in main(), before the feed
    (never opened in this mode); then no-op window procedures (the hangup
    ones, nhwindows_headless()), so nothing waits for a key; chdirx()
    without the record check; a scratch playground under $TMPDIR, as for
    a replay, removed however the run ends (atexit()), so the real
    playground is only read and an open game's lock and level files are
    never seen; no options file or NETHACKOPTIONS; the seed from sysconf's
    SEED, or else the first line of standard input, taken as SEED would be
    (hidden, and allowed to start with "hidden#"); the hero named
    "layouts" without plnamesuffix(); sysconf's RECORDFILE, DUMPLOGFILE,
    MSGHANDLER and CRASHREPORTURL, and NH_RECORD, NH_SEEDFUZZ, NH_STATELOG
    and NH_FEEDCHECK ignored. newgame() runs as usual, then
    layout_dump_run() makes every level as the fuzzer's base pass does,
    each with the Fort Ludios portal not yet placed. The output is built
    in memory and written only on success, to a file it makes with mode
    0600 (an existing plain file is made 0600 before it is emptied, and
    left alone, with the run refused, if it can't be; a device or a pipe
    is left as it is; only a file it made is removed if writing fails).
    With FILE "-", the original standard output is kept for the dump and
    descriptor 1 points at standard error, so nothing else reaches it; a
    reader that goes away is a failed write.

  o It refuses, exiting 1 with one line on standard error and no output,
    with permissions the player lacks (real and effective IDs differ), a
    build with VAR_PLAYGROUND or without HANGUPHANDLING, wizard mode, data
    files that can't be identified (no DLB), an invalid sysconf SEED or
    seed on standard input, and no seed at all. A panic or impossible()
    during the run stops it the same way, and any other ending without
    the dump written exits 1 (layout_dump_status()).

  o A seeded game ignores the ROGUEOPTS environment variable (roguename()):
    its name for the Rogue level's ghost would both rename the ghost and,
    by skipping the name's random draws, shift that level's layout stream.
    Unseeded games keep NetHack 5.0's behaviour.

  o Reading a keyframe or a dump:
      - Cells are i = y * 80 + x. "terr" and "vis" runs go on in i past
        the ends of rows; each of a "terr" run's four strings is as long
        as the run, and "vis" [i, n] is cells i to i + n - 1.
      - With "layout" null (an unseeded game, wizard mode) the base is
        all stone: typ 'A', flags 'A', lit '0', horiz '0' (packed 0).
      - "g" has "gw" characters per cell from A-Z a-z 0-9 + / (values
        0-63); with "gw" 2 a cell's value is 64 * first + second. The
        value indexes "sym", ordered by first appearance in "g" by
        increasing i, then in "scr".
      - "special" is the special level's name ("" for an ordinary one),
        "dname" the dungeon's full name; Water and Air are the levels
        whose "special" is "water" or "air" (the dump's "moves": 1).
      - A keyframe's traps, engravings, stairs and rooms are the level's
        as they are, in the game's own order: its traps include the Fort
        Ludios portal in a vault, where the Fort Ludios level's portal
        really leads, and the webs under room monsters. The dump's are
        the layout's, sorted by cell, without those.
      - Dump lines have no "t" or "a"; the hash-only form's end hash is
        the full form's.
      - Values:
          branch type  0 stairs, 1 no stairs at end1, 2 none at end2,
                       3 magic portals
          typ          0 stone, 1 vertical wall, 2 horizontal wall, 3-6
                       corners (top left, top right, bottom left, bottom
                       right), 7 cross wall, 8-11 T walls (up, down,
                       left, right), 12 drawbridge wall, 13 tree, 14
                       secret door, 15 secret corridor, 16 pool, 17 moat,
                       18 water, 19 raised drawbridge, 20 lava, 21 lava
                       wall, 22 iron bars, 23 door, 24 corridor, 25 room,
                       26 stairs, 27 ladder, 28 fountain, 29 throne, 30
                       sink, 31 grave, 32 altar, 33 ice, 34 lowered
                       drawbridge, 35 air, 36 cloud
          flags        by typ: doors 1 broken, 2 open, 4 closed, 8
                       locked, 16 trapped (0: no door); walls and stone
                       0-7 wall mode, 8 undiggable, 16 not passable;
                       secret doors 0-7 wall mode, 8 locked, 16 trapped;
                       altars 1 chaotic, 2 neutral, 4 lawful, 8 shrine,
                       16 sanctum; fountains 1 looted, 2 warned; sinks 1
                       pudding, 2 dish washer, 4 ring taken; thrones and
                       trees 1 looted, trees 2 swarm; stairs and ladders
                       1 up, 2 down; drawbridges 0-3 facing north, south,
