It builds without new warnings normally, without USE_ISAAC64 (the three
warnings about set_random() there are upstream's), with AddressSanitizer
and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, for sfctool, and for Windows (x64,
cross-compiled with MinGW-w64 and sys/windows/GNUmakefile, extra warnings
on).


  +-----------------------+
  | C2. How it's checked  |
  +-----------------------+

On every push, CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) builds the game with the
Linux and macOS hints from a clean tree (so build= is honest), checks all
dat/*.lua scripts with the bundled Lua compiler, and builds on Linux
x86_64 and aarch64 and macOS x86_64 and arm64; on Linux x86_64
it runs the history fuzzer (B10, test/seedfuzz.py) over a few dozen
seeds, startup and saved-seed checks (test/seedcheck.py), the recording
tests (B11, test/replaytest.py, with and without
signals, and test/recordfail.py), panic-save checks (test/panictest.py),
save refusal and recovery checks (test/savecheck.py),
cached Lua diagnostic checks (test/luatest.py), running-state checks
after travel and digging (test/turncounter.py, with gdb),
live-feed checks (B12, test/feedtest.py), layout dump and keyframe
reconstruction checks (B14, test/layouttest.py) and managed-session
checks (B13, test/sessiontest.py, test/launchtest.py);
it also checks the harnesses' failure detection and temporary configuration
cleanup (test/harnesstest.py, test/sysconftest.py);
on every platform it builds sfctool
and checks its tag parser (test/sftagstest.py), packages a release tarball
and, with the compiled-in playground removed, makes every level of one
seed from the unpacked tarball and writes its layout hashes
(--layout-hashes), which a final job requires to be the same bytes on
every platform. Both Linux builds use Ubuntu 22.04
containers (glibc 2.35), so a newer runner does not raise the runtime
requirement. The supported Linux baseline is glibc 2.35 or newer.
Separate Debian 12 and 13 jobs run both x86_64 and aarch64 tarballs and
make every level of one seed, and write its layout hashes, with only
runtime libraries installed.
A v* tag publishes the tarballs after these compatibility checks pass.

What the checks establish:

  o The fuzzer makes every level under each perturbation in B10 and allows
    only that perturbation's documented differences, the fingerprints and
    each level's wandering monster timeline included. It does catch leaks:
    hashing an object's monster-id field into the fingerprint, or reading
    the high score list for a doppelganger's form, fails it.
    Missing passes, empty baseline levels, differing level sets and
    incomplete fingerprints and the all-zero missing-fingerprint sentinel
    fail before comparisons start.

  o Startup checks compare the character, attributes, kit, pet and observed
    object appearances across player options in normal, explore and wizard
    modes, and check the documented handicap exceptions. Saved public,
    hidden and unseeded games keep their seed under changed configuration;
    a hidden game's new level after restore matches its original seed's
    layout. Hidden records need the correct seed to replay, and invalid
    server seeds cannot fall back to a player's seed (test/seedcheck.py).

  o A replay checks out only if every key, system seed and signal of the
    recorded game reproduces every checkpoint digest; malformed records
    (truncated, wrong lengths, unknown entries, out-of-range values,
    embedded '\0's) are rejected.
    Wizard/explore random tests also require their requested session,
    save/restore and signal paths, including an interrupt declined during
    play; the final quit interrupt cannot satisfy that check. A shorter
    verified record fails the coverage check. Normal-mode death is
    reported as reduced coverage, including while waiting at disclosure.

  o A game whose record can't be written ends saved, and a replay can't
    write into the playground (test/recordfail.py).

  o A layout dump is the same bytes every time and in both forms agrees
    with itself, ignores options files, NETHACKOPTIONS and ROGUEOPTS, runs
    in a read-only playground beside an open game without touching
    either, and refuses what it can't be made for. Feeds of games seeded
    as a race server seeds them, saved and restored, fold back to the
    true terrain, map, screen and view at every "chk" line, against the
    dump's layouts, and to every keyframe on a level already described,
    hero, inventory, discoveries, objects and monsters included
    (test/layouttest.py). The walks wait for a command boundary after
    the last key read, by action count, so in-turn prompts and multi-turn
    updates cannot finish the wait; a failed wait stops the walk. The
    harness checks cover these waits and their Escape recovery
    (test/harnesstest.py).

  o A normal seeded launch with strict policy and terminal setup
    produces complete snapshots and verified recordings when hung up from
    gameplay, menus, line input and death disclosure, and a snapshot taken
    while it waits in the middle of a turn finds nothing the feed hasn't
    already written (test/launchtest.py). This checks the native game
    interface, not VM isolation or networking.

Checked by hand, not by CI:

  o A killed game: a session ended with SIGKILL, the recover utility run
    on its checkpoint files, and the game restored and finished, replays
    as "cut off" then "verified", the replay recovering the files itself
    between the two sessions.

  o Word size and libc don't matter: a 32-bit musl build (another qsort())
    writes byte for byte the same fuzzer output as 64-bit glibc.

  o AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer builds run the fuzzer,
    the replay tests, and recovery of a game killed with SIGKILL (seeded
    or not, with or without a seed option or sysconf SEED) cleanly.

  o Save files: an unseeded game's save restores in NetHack 5.0 and vice
    versa; NetHack 5.0 refuses a seeded game's save ("Configuration
    incompatibility"); both convert through sfctool and back; a level
    made after a restore has the fingerprint it would have had without
    one; an invalid saved seed is refused without losing the save.

  o Hidden seeds: the digest and the fingerprints of a game under a
    sysconf SEED match a reference game given the same seed as a player's
    option; an attack that recovers a seed from fingerprints salted with a
    plain sub-seed finds nothing against the chained salt, and one guess
    against a fingerprint costs the whole chain.

  o Records: a new game never overwrites an existing record or writes
    through a symbolic link; a restored game whose record is a link is
    saved again and ends; a hangup at the legacy text, in a long search,
    and ^C between turns all replay.

Not yet run anywhere: Windows. CI only builds it (MSYS2, x64, from
sys/windows/build-msys2.txt), allowed to fail, and keeps the result out
of the releases.
